- Group numbers ran from 01–99 (00 is not valid). They were assigned in an internal pattern (not simply sequential) for administrative purposes — they do not represent a geographic area or other personal attribute.
- Since June 25, 2011, the SSA uses a randomized assignment method. After that change, the area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate where or when an SSN was issued.

Validity notes:
- Any SSN with 000 for the area, 00 for the group, or 0000 for the serial is invalid.
- If your form asks for “last four,” you only need the SSSS (the final four digits).

If you want, tell me exactly what the form is asking (for example, “enter middle two digits” or “enter last four digits”) and I can confirm which part to enter without you typing your full SSN here. If you need an official source, I can point you to the SSA’s page on SSN structure and randomization.

