Short answer: those middle two digits are the SSN’s "group number." They don’t identify you personally (like age or state) — they were an internal bookkeeping code the Social Security Administration used to break each area’s numbers into manageable blocks.

More detail:
- SSN format is AAA‑GG‑SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the issuing office/region),
  - GG = group number (the middle two digits),
  - SSSS = serial number (the last four).
- The group number (01–99) was used by the SSA to organize issuance within an area number; it’s an administrative batching code, not a geographic or personal identifier.
- Since SSA’s Randomization policy (started June 25, 2011), area and group numbers no longer convey geographic information — SSNs are basically randomly assigned now.

A quick security note: don’t share your full SSN in public or unsecured channels. If the form only needs the last four digits, provide only those unless the form specifically requires the full SSN and you’re on a trusted, secure site.

If you want, tell me what the form is asking (full SSN vs. last four) and I can help confirm how to enter it or what to watch out for.

