First, a quick privacy note: I won't store or use your SSN, and I recommend not posting the full number in messages or public places. I also noticed the full number and the “last four” you gave don’t match — double-check what you intended to share.

What the middle section of a U.S. Social Security number means (general explanation)
- SSNs are formatted AAA-GG-SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the issuing office/geography)
  - GG = group number (the middle three digits)
  - SSSS = serial number (last four digits)
- The middle part (the group number, GG):
  - Is an administrative identifier used by the Social Security Administration to help organize and issue numbers.
  - Historically it ranged from 01 to 99 and didn’t encode personal information like age, race, or exact location — it was just part of the SSA’s issuance scheme.
  - Note: “00” is not a valid group number.
- Important change: Since 2011 the SSA moved to “randomization” for new SSNs. That means area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate where or when an SSN was issued.

Safety tips
- Only give your full SSN when absolutely required and to trusted, verified parties (employers, government agencies, banks).
- Make sure web forms use HTTPS and that the request is legitimate before entering a full SSN.
- If a form only needs identity verification, often the last four digits suffice — ask the requester if that’s acceptable.
- Never send a full SSN in an unsecured email or public message.

If you want, I can:
- Help you reword a question to the organization asking whether they need the full SSN or if the last four will do.
- Review the form’s instructions (you can paste the non-sensitive parts) and suggest what to enter.

