Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick correction first: a U.S. SSN is formatted AAA‑GG‑SSSS (9 digits). The middle section (the "GG") is a two‑digit group number.

What the group number means
- Historically it was an administrative sequencing code used (together with the first three "area" digits) to help the Social Security Administration allocate and track blocks of numbers.  
- The group number itself does not encode personal information (no birthdate, no state of birth, no citizenship status). It was simply part of the SSA’s issuance pattern.  
- Since SSA’s randomization policy (started June 2011), area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate where or when an SSN was issued.

A couple of important notes
- Fields of all zeros are not valid SSNs: area 000, group 00, or serial 0000 are not issued.  
- Share your full SSN only when absolutely required and only over secure, trusted channels. If a form accepts just the last four, that’s safer. Check for HTTPS and legitimate requester identity before entering your SSN.

If you want, I can:
- Check the form for signs it’s secure (what URL or service is it?), or  
- Help you decide whether it’s safe to provide the full SSN or if you should give only the last four.

