Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: the middle two digits are the "group number" and are just an administrative sequencing code — they don't encode your age, birth location, or any personal detail.

A bit more detail:
- SSNs use the format AAA‑GG‑SSSS (area – group – serial).
- The group number (GG, two digits from 01–99) was historically used by the Social Security Administration to break an area’s numbers into smaller blocks for processing and issuance. It’s an internal sequence, not personal information.
- Since SSA began randomizing SSN issuance in 2011, area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate geography or timing.
- Important: SSA never issues numbers where any whole segment is all zeros (area 000, group 00, or serial 0000). So a number shown as 000-00-XXXX is almost certainly a placeholder, typo, or otherwise invalid.

Security note: avoid posting your full SSN publicly. If the form only needs the last four digits, enter just those. If you want, tell me which digits the form asks for (e.g., last four) and I can help confirm what to type without you sharing the full number here.

