Quick safety note: please don’t share your full SSN in messages or public places. I won’t store it, but it’s best to redact (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) when asking about forms.

Brief answer about the middle digits
- Social Security numbers are written AAA-GG-SSSS.
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to geographic region)
  - GG = group number (the middle two digits)
  - SSSS = serial number (last four)
- The middle two digits (the group number) were an internal administrative code the Social Security Administration used to organize and issue numbers within an area. They do not encode personal data such as your age or county of birth.
- Since SSA’s 2011 “randomization” change, area and group assignments no longer indicate geography or follow the old issuance patterns—now the numbers are essentially random for privacy/security.

Other practical points
- A few patterns are invalid: area = 000, group = 00, or serial = 0000 are not valid SSNs.
- Many forms ask only for the last four; that’s commonly accepted because it’s less sensitive.
- Only enter your full SSN on trusted, secure sites/forms (look for HTTPS/lock icon) and never send it in plain email or text unless explicitly required and secure.
- If you’re unsure whether a form legitimately needs your full SSN, I can help you check the request wording or draft a reply asking for a secure submission method.

