Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I won’t repeat or use the SSN you pasted. For your safety, avoid sharing the full SSN in messages.

Quick answer
- The “middle section” of a U.S. Social Security number (the part in the middle of the usual format XXX-XX-XXXX) is the two-digit group number.

More detail
- SSN format: AAA-GG-SSSS (three-digit area, two-digit group, four-digit serial).
- Group number (GG): originally used by the Social Security Administration for administrative ordering/allocation within an area. Group numbers run 01–99 and are not a checksum or a personal code.
- Area number (AAA): used to indicate the state/location of issuance prior to June 25, 2011. Since SSA’s randomization policy (2011), area and group numbers no longer reliably indicate geography or issuance order.
- Serial number (SSSS): a sequential number within the group (0001–9999).

Common validity rules (what you might see on forms or validations)
- Area (first three) cannot be 000. Historically 666 and 900–999 were not issued for standard SSNs.
- Group (middle two) cannot be 00.
- Serial (last four) cannot be 0000.

Safety tips when a form asks for your SSN
- Only enter it on a trusted site (look for HTTPS and a reputable organization).
- Ask if the form really needs the full SSN — many places accept just the last 4 digits.
- Don’t send your full SSN over unsecured email, chat, or text.
- If unsure, call the organization using a number from their official website to confirm.

If you want, I can:
- Help read the form text/field to confirm whether it’s asking for the middle two digits or something else.
- Suggest how to respond if a site asks for the entire SSN and you’d prefer not to provide it.

