I won’t repeat your full SSN here for security, but I can explain what the middle three digits mean.

Quick overview of SSN structure
- SSNs are written AAA-GG-SSSS.
  - AAA = area number (originally showed the issuing region/state; since 2011 it no longer does).
  - GG = group number (the middle three digits you asked about).
  - SSSS = serial number (unique within that group).

What the middle three digits (the “group number”) mean
- The middle three digits are commonly called the group number.
- Historically the Social Security Administration used group numbers to organize and sequence issuance within each area number. There was a pattern (odd numbers 01–09, then even 10–98) used for administrative assignment — but that pattern was about processing order, not personal information about you.
- Since the SSA switched to randomized SSN assignment on June 25, 2011, the area and group numbers no longer indicate geographic region or any personal trait. They’re effectively just part of a unique identifier now.
- In short: the middle three digits are an internal sequencing code used by SSA; they don’t convey meaningful personal information like your location, birth date, or status.

Safety tips when entering your SSN
- Only enter your SSN on secure, trusted sites or official forms. Look for HTTPS and a padlock in the browser.
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in email, chat, or public places. If a form only needs identity verification, often the last four digits are sufficient.
