Short answer: the middle two digits of a U.S. Social Security number are the "group number." Historically they were part of the internal issuance/order system and did not encode any personal trait; since 2011 the SSA randomized most assignments, so those digits no longer indicate location or other personal information.

More detail:
- SSN format is written as AAA-GG-SSSS:
  - AAA = area number (originally tied to the state or region where the SSN was issued; since 2011 this is randomized for newly issued numbers)
  - GG = group number (two digits used by the SSA to help organize issuance within an area — it’s an administrative code, not a code for a person’s characteristics)
  - SSSS = serial number (four digits unique within that area/group)
- The group number ranges from 01 to 99 and was used to control the order of assignment; it does not reveal your age, income, or whereabouts.
- Since the SSA’s randomization policy (started in 2011), area and group numbers don’t reliably reflect geography or issuance order for newer numbers.

Privacy/security tips
- Never share your full SSN in public messages or insecure forms. If someone legitimately needs your SSN, verify the requestor and that the website/form is secure (HTTPS, reputable organization).
- When possible, provide only the last four digits instead of the full SSN.
- If you’re unsure whether a form is legitimate, I can help you check the form or wording (don’t paste your SSN); I can also suggest alternatives the requester might accept.

